And the-- we stayed there for two nights, in this place, in the synagogue. And they kept us guards. You couldn't go out. We couldn't do nothing. I mean, it was on the second-- third night-- third day, they took us, put us on the train.

And it was 100 in one train-- no, not 100, there were 60 people in one-- in one train, a goods train. Close the door, and that's it.

INT: You're with your-- the rest of your family?

EK: I was with my family, yes. My family, mine family, my-- my uncle-- my auntie's, not the uncle, the auntie. Because our uncle was taken with the boys, with the men. And the-- the only family is some and a-- another uncle. He-- he was-- was over age, so they-- he didn't-- they didn't take him. He came with us.

So, a few of us in-- in the train-- there were one, three-- one, two, three, four, four uncles-- four families. And we were together in one-- one train, and a few other people too.

They took us away. For three days, we, we traveled in the train, till we came to Auschwitz.

INT: Did you have anything to eat on the train?

EK: Yes, they gave us just some meat, some salamis. I mean some bread. And every day, we used to get something to eat. But not-- you know, no-- you couldn't go to toilet. You had to-- everything, you had to do in this-- in the inside, in a box, box there.

And after three day, we arrive to Auschwitz. We saw the-- so they took us on the platform. So they divide us, the men, extra, and children, with the-- with the-- with the women. Just-- and we stayed there with the platform.

Straightaway, the SS, with the-- with the guns, we no move, no nothing, only things you left behind, only what as you are. We stayed in the platform about half an hour, something like this. We staying and staying and staying. They said, back-- back to the-- in the train. They were goings. And we went back-- back again. We traveled another three days.

INT: So.

EK: We don't know where.

INT: So did the whole train move on?

EK: The whole train, all the people back again, and move on again.

INT: Do you know why that happened.

EK: We don't know why. I think-- well, my thinking, later on, because it didn't have it ready. The gas chambers wasn't ready yet. In 1942, May, they didn't have ready nothing yet. Only gas chamber started later on, middle of the year. Not middle of year, I mean September, something like this. And so they just pushed us around.

INT: What was it like when you arrived there? Like after-- did they open the doors at all on the train trip, in the three days?

EK: No, no, it's all closed. All the doors was closed. Only, when we had to go out, they open the door, and we went out.

INT: What was that like the first time?

EK: Very, very, very bad feeling, horrible feeling, because the guns. And, from all the sides was gun, with the guns watching that somebody went trip, just try to shoot, something this. So that everybody frightened, very frightened.

The kids, we-- we-- as a child, we were very frightened. We stick to the mother. What was going on, we didn't know. We were very fright.

They took us back on the train. And we went to another place. We were in I don't know, was-- maybe it was Majdanek.

INT: Can-- can I just ask, the girls from your village at Auschwitz, what-- what was that? You saw them?

EK: We only saw them screaming, mother, this and this. But we didn't know what was going on. Because when the gate was closed, they couldn't come out. It was, you know, the big gates, the iron gates. Like in every camp, there was the same-- same gates, the iron, written Arbeit-- Arbeit Macht Das Leben Frei. And we saw them, some of the girls.

But you-- we couldn't-- we couldn't move. We couldn't. We had to stay. And the girls are stayed there, behind the gates. But the, the-- then they moved them, the girls, this SS, something, from the-- from the gates. So--

INT: Did you actually recognize them yourself?

EK: Some of them, yeah. I recognize some of them girls. Yeah, which-- yeah, from the-- our city, yeah.

INT: Did you think that maybe that's where your sisters were?

EK: Yeah. Because same-- same group, yeah. But the-- we couldn't do nothing.

INT: What was it like, like standing there, just wondering?

EK: [SIGHS] Oh, it was very bad. Because [SIGHS] we didn't know what was going on. You saw there's a factory there, maybe, or something like this there. But, you know, they made-- the-- they made us.

The Germans made us so thinking that we going to factory, we going to work. But was not, wasn't like this. It wasn't like this. For all promises, the promise there will be good and everything, but they-- you know, it happens, we didn't know.

But we came to the place-- very dark, very frightful. Then we came to-- after three days, they decided we going to-- they, they dropped us in a place, in Deblin. And there was--

INT: So-- so you got back on the-- on the train, and you just went for three days?

EK: Yeah, for three days. And then we came to a place. It was Deblin. So every-- all, everybody out, out, out, quick. So put us in a march, and to march. Was-- was three SS men. We were about 5,000 people, I mean, kids and their family, yeah?

So the-- it was about two kilometers from the-- from the ghetto. So we had to march. And, you know, with the-- with the guns in the hand. And anybody was trying to get out, they tried to shoot, try to, you know, show the gun, that they were going to shoot him. But they didn't kill nothing on them, on the things, on the way.

INT: Did you--

EK: They came--

INT: --see any of the locals on the way?

EK: No. Nobody. Was only-- you couldn't see nobody, no people. You just kept going. And then it came to the ghetto, then we saw the-- what's going on-- just filthy, neglected, dirty.

And they put in one room. It was four or five families in one room. Sleep on the floor, you know, next to each other. But then I just, you know, I came to the-- we-- you know.

And the next day, we had to go out.

INT: So what time did-- did you arrive at night?

EK: No, we arrived in daytime, morning. It-- it took us-- or maybe we arrived at night, but, in the morning, we, we, we walked from, from the-- from the train to the ghetto.

INT: Right.

EK: And came there.

INT: Did you know the other five families that were in the-- the room with you?

EK: Yes, yes. We could-- we didn't stay together. It was not a house, you know? It was a-- in the ghetto, and it's a rooms. It was such a awful place. And the next morning, they-- we had to go out, men out, go to work.

What was the work? Break stones, make such a-- you know, stone, break the stones, and put them in on the road, to make the footpath of the main road in-- in ghetto. In ghetto, not the outside.

And all kind of just-- just to make people work, to go up there and do the same work, again and again and again. But I stayed in ghetto for a few days.

INT: What were you doing during those days?

EK: I was working, also, on the rocks, putting rocks on the-- on the floor, on the-- on the ground. After a few days, I-- they came. A person came, you know. They needed men for work on the land in the-- on the airport, on the air force, German air force.

So me and my cousin went to get the work, because then, you know, you get a bit more food, you know? So what we-- we worked all day. At nighttime, we came back to ghetto.

INT: And so how did you-- how did you go out?

EK: Oh, it was a guards. They-- they took us there, with the guards, to the airport. That's about one, two kilometers.

INT: Did you walk?

EK: Walk? Yes, walk all the way, or we'll walk then back again.

INT: Did you need passes or anything to get out?

EK: No, no pass. It was nothing. No passes. And no, no names, no nothing-- numbers.

INT: Right. Do you remember what your number was?

EK: Oh, I don't know I remember now. I mean it's-- I don't even remember none of them. Because everywhere, every place, you had different numbers, always change numbers.

And we-- then, all of sudden, then they-- all of sudden, they needed-- they put us in a-- in gang for work on the roads, pick and shovel. So-- so when we gather, they put us in these gangs, to put us in the-- in the camp. We didn't go more to the ghetto. We went to the camp.

INT: So did you get to say goodbye, like, to your auntie?

EK: No, no, not at all. Just in-- in-- in morning before. But once in the camp, I just couldn't, couldn't do nothing. They were in the ghetto, and we were in a camp.

INT: So what was it like that? You didn't-- did you know that you were leaving the ghetto for the last time?

EK: No, no. Oh, yeah. No, no, I didn't know this time. But you see, when I was working in the-- in the camp, the four days before the liquidation of the ghetto, they put us-- send us back to the ghetto, me and my cousin.

A lot of-- but, but there was very weak people, I mean little boys or somebody too old, they send them back to the ghetto-- four days. Because they knew that's going to be liquidation. So they put us out in the-- back to the ghetto.

But when we came back to the ghetto, and first day, so next day, we-- he-- he apply-- we went to work on the land again, what we used to before. So we came back to-- back to the ghetto, and back to-- back again at night. And we came back to ghetto.

When was the liquidation, we were on the land. That was about 10th of October-- 10th or 15 October. And there was a liquidation of the ghetto. We were on the-- working on the land.

When we came, finish the work on land, we heard that it-- what was going on in ghetto, because the Polish people they said-- told us what was going on, that's a liquidation is going on.

INT: How did they describe it? Did they just say, liquidation?

EK: [SIGHS] Putting people on the Appellplatz and put them on the trains. And there-- they went from house to house. Anybody left behind got killed on the spot. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] but just, because we-- we were on the land. We-- we-- we-- we heard from the Polish people, from the not-Jewish, from Polish people what they're doing, that they, they finish the ghetto.

And we cried. I mean, we couldn't do nothing. At night, we-- there-- we didn't-- we were not in a camp. We were not in the ghetto. We were on the field, all night, just to decide what was going on with us, with this group what was on the land.

The-- the head of the land, which of-- that call this, you know, of the land, he-- he ask the SS just to let us go in the camp. That we need-- he need us for, for the work. So-- so they decided the next day the-- we worked. We stayed overnight in the-- where the cows, you know, how they call this? In a-- where the cows lived, in all those places, we stayed overnight there.

INT: What was the weather then? What was it? What season was it?

EK: October. It was a bad season, cold. So the next morning, we went back to land to work. And they let us back in the camp next night. So we-- we-- we-- we were already settled, because, by then-- 10 days after when the first liquidation was, there's still some left in the ghetto. So the-- what was left, they took them in another ghetto. And also, after, because liquidation, you know, there was the liquidation, so they was left over. They took them there and send them to Treblinka.

INT: So did you-- and where-- where was your auntie?

EK: She was taken in the first--

INT: In the first one?

EK: --first one, yeah. Big-- big-- wait a minute. Yes, I want to say, also, she went with the first liquidation. They just-- they shot and they killed everybody, over 1,000 people in the ghetto.

Next day, we had to go pick up the bodies, from the-- you know, bodies-- and put on the mass grave on the Jewish cemetery. I found bodies everywhere, you know? There was some dumdum bullets. That's a bullet, when you get in the body, it open the wound and everything. So we-- just that we have to go. We have to go and collect this and put them on the-- in the grave.

INT: Did you notice-- did you recognize anybody?

EK: No. Just you didn't recognize nobody. It was-- you know, I- I was very, [SIGHS] very upset. It was very, very bad. Why did we have to do it? Because nobody would.

You know, they made us to do it. We-- they took us off the land and, one day, to pick up all the bodies and put in the grave and cover it and back to the camp.

INT: How did you move them?

EK: By hand. Pick up the-- by, you know, feet and by hands, two-- two-- two people would. And it was in a-- in a-- in a truck, a-- you know the-- not a car, in a horse and buggy, used it to carry this.

INT: Did you do that with Jacob?

EK: Yes. We together. Not only me and him. I mean we have about a-- a few hundred boys. Only boys used-- came to that, to do those job. The girls were left behind in the camp.

INT: Was that just one day?

EK: Just one day, yes.

INT: What happened at the end of the day? Where did you go from there?

EK: [SIGHS] Well, I went back to camp. We stayed overnight, you know? Just everybody was crying, upset. But we couldn't do nothing. Next day, we had to go back to work.

INT: Were you able to wash? Like what were the conditions like at the camp?

EK: We had there-- yes, we had to wash once a week. We had a Entlausung. We had a shower. And you had-- put your clothes in a-- in a-- in a like a heat, to kill the-- the, the lice and all those things, to not to have this thing, you know? So every week, once a week, we had it done. It's compulsory, it was.

INT: Where they still-- were they still your clothes?

EK: And yes. But you got it from, from them. Because, one, you know, we used to get the clothes from the-- from the-- from Majdanek-- no, from Treblinka came the--

What you-- what you got, you have to take it. You know, it, it was a very cold winter. I remember in 1943-- '42-'43, winter was very cold. We didn't have no coat, overcoat, no nothing. So, and it's-- but we had to work on the land.

Used to work on the land for the-- for the air force potatoes. I used to put-- dig the potatoes in the cellars. You have to take it out. And they go on this. When there's snow, put the turf on the-- on the ground, that's in summer. That's when the winter finishes, so the land became able to use it again.

It was also, you know, it was very bad, very-- from beginning-- at first, yes, I forgot to tell you. When I used to work pick and shovel, used to make the roads. We had the-- you had to-- you had your place. You have to fix it. I mean dig it.

And then, you have to finish it, the work. Yeah, you get smacked if you didn't do it. If you stays for a few minute, you get belted. You know, it was very bad.

But on the land, we worked under the Polish guard-- no guards, a foreman. You know, SS was outside. But inside was the Polish people, you know. So it wasn't so bad. But, but still, you have to work.

But it's seven days a week, non-stop. On-- even in the winter, when it's snow, had to go shovel the snow from the airfield too. It was 38 degrees. I mean-- I mean 20 degrees below 0, 28 degrees-- degrees below 0.

And the wind from the-- when it came the snow, you know, the wind carried. You had-- you had to work. It was very cold. And, but--

INT: How-- how did-- how-- were you used to doing such work like that, like constant physical work?

EK: No. But there, I had to do it.

INT: How did you cope with that?

EK: Oh, I coped it. I mean-- I mean I used to-- but I used to get the easier work. When I was work on the land-- on the-- on the road, I used to do it. With the wagons, I used to be the driver, driving the things that, and open the-- the-- you know, just the earth. I had the easier job there.

INT: How did you manage that? How did you get those jobs?

EK: Because the-- the German fellow, he was teaching-- I was a little boy, very-- you know? Just a small size, skinny, and he-- he-- he just was not a SS man. He was a German fellow. And he-- he just had a pity on me. So he taught me to-- to drive it, so I'm able to get a job.

But yeah, everywhere, you know, even I was working on the land, also I used to-- I mean. That's in-- in winter was very bad, was very cold. But in the summer, I used to work on the land, pick up the weeds by hand. You know, everything was done by hand, the weeds or work on the flower things. Everybody there, you know, had to work.

Potatoes, you know, take the rotten ones from the-- from the good ones. This was in a-- how do they call this? In a like a cave they made special, you know? But they keep the potatoes there, for winter, to-- not to get frozen.

INT: Did you ever want like steal potatoes or?

EK: We did. We did. I mean, not-- how do you mean, steal? We used to carry-- we had one fellow, a Polish fellow, not-- non-Jewish. He-- he came as a foreman. He was a foreman. But he was a--

So he used to-- we used to bring him potatoes from the land in the-- in the shirts. And he took it home, in his-- not me, my cousin, and a few boys used to give him the potatoes. And he used to bring us, the next day, some cooked potatoes in the morning. Yeah, it was-- but it was very risky. If they would catch you, just you out.

INT: Yeah.

EK: It was also a case of one boy, you know. He was-- he was pinching soap from the Germans, from the cellar. They caught him. They hanged him during the night. Was, you know, it was risky, very risky. Anytime they catch you with something, there you go. Was no-- no, you know? They made there for hanging. What you call this?

INT: Gallows.

EK: The things for hanging. And just we're standing there. We-- we all had to watch when it happened, you know, the chair? Once, I remember, when it-- from beginning, when I came from-- that's from beginning at the-- in the camp.

In 1942 was a case, six boys didn't come to work. They were in ghetto. And two boys were still in-- in camp, which didn't go to work, but they came to camp. So he-- the SS man-- came SS man, and he took them out. A-- a first one got 50 lashes. He had to count 50 lashes with a-- you know, on his back. After 50 lashes, he took the gun, in front of everybody, and shot him.

And you know, one fell in front of me. I was-- because I was a little boy, so they put me in the back. Because one fell in the front. He collapsed, fainted. So they put me in the front. When I saw that, I had this-- you know? So when he-- he shot--

He took the other, you know, the second one, he didn't shot in front of us, he shot him inside, in the side of the-- you know, outside, just from the block. Next to the block, he shot him.

Then I had to take-- four boys went there to dig the grave and put the bodies there.

INT: Was that the first time you'd seen that?

EK: That's the first one, first time when I saw the shooting, yeah. Or, in the ghetto, I wasn't there, but there, in ghetto, was also shooting. Yeah, but I wasn't there. You know, when found that one men had a coal, to put fire. And he just came home, and he has a coal. And the SS man just passed and shot him on the-- on his-- on the-- in their place, on the spot.

INT: So you heard about that one?

EK: Yeah, I heard of this one. But this one in the-- in the camp, I saw this, my life.

INT: And the camp was at Deblin, as well, was it?

EK: I beg your pardon?

INT: The camp, was it Deblin, as well?

EK: Yeah, that's in Deblin. Yeah, that's everything in Deblin, yeah. And sometime-- once was a case also, there was a Russian camp over the hill. Other side of the hill was a camp from Russian prisoners. And they had music. They made the music, from their pots and the things. And they made this singing.

So the the SS men all thought that we have a-- we have a radio in the camp. So he took-- he say, when we came from work, straightaway, every 10 men on the side. And the SS man was watching us, standing. Another SS, two, two guards were standing and watching us.

Who they took out? All the young ones, the kids, every 10 person, the kids. So we were every 10 person standing there. Through our luck, the Russians start to sing again. And the SS man, which was guarding us, he heard that, that' it's not--

So, he give them-- he said to the officer, that's not-- it's not a radio in the camp. It's the Russians are singing there. So they let, let us out. Let us back to the camp, between the people.

INT: And so it-- was it only boys at the camp?

EK: It was mostly boys. See, that was two groups. One was there working on the trains, on the rails, fixing the rails. Was men only. There was, in our group, where I was working on the land, there was a lot more girls than men.

In this camp, we-- we have over-- over 3,000 people. Well, it was-- or there were one, two, three blocks, and were over 3,000 people. The girls have their own block, and the rest of them mixed in with us.

And everyone, you know, every morning, with the appell, you had to count. So we stayed from 6 o'clock in the morning till after seven o'clock, before they count us. And then we go march. Everyone has different group-- work.

In 1943--

INT: OK, we just better pause.

