INT: OK, so you were telling us about the-- the makeup of the camps.

EK: Yeah, In 1943, it was the group which was working on the-- on the fixing it-- the rails. They finished the work, so they sent them to-- they'd liquidate them too, in 1943.

In this, in-- in Deblin, there were six different work-- I mean, different camps, labor camps. All of them, when they finished the work, they were liquidated.

To [NON-ENGLISH], because the air force, the German Air Force, asked the SS to keep us on the-- on work, so we stayed there. We worked all the time, every day-- I mean, to work, working on the land, on the roads-- all kinds of work-- what the German Air Force needed-- so we did it.

We were left from 3,000-- which were taken about 1,000-- over 1,000 men, which were taken in 1943, they left the rest, the other ones left behind. We stayed there till 194-- July, 1944, because 1944 is when the Russians started to come.

So the-- so the-- in '4-- in some time in July, came a fellow from a-- a German SS. And he spoke very nice-- that he has got another camp for us in western Poland, in very good homes and everything.

So the people thought, oh, gee, maybe, yes. And when we-- two weeks before we were-- we were taken to Tschenstochau. There was sent a group, also, from our camp to the camp there, to the Tschenstochau. It was a munition factory.

INT: Could we just--

EK: In, till 1944, we worked on the land in-- in-- in Deblin. In 1944, in August-- July-- we were taken by train to Tschenstochau. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And Tschenstochau was a very bad place.

For-- from the beginning, I used to work on the steel industry, steel works for four weeks. I-- it was-- wasn't so bad, this camp. There was not-- no-- no-- no-- no hitting, no-- no-- smacking, nothing-- only work, hard work.

INT: What sort of work were you doing?

EK: I was working where this-- when the oven was open. And I had to go with the water to cool it off-- this, the iron. And after that, came the fellas who'd break up the-- from the steel.

Then we have to carry this steel, put it in-- drop it in the wagon. I was there for four weeks.

INT: Was, was Jacob there with you?

EK: Jacob was with me, yes. He was-- he was in different work. He was working with the nails, on something like this.

INT: And would you come together at night? Would you be staying together?

EK: Yes, we were staying together in the same camp, yes. Finally, when I-- when we came to this camp, first, the first night, there was only men there. Only a few girls there were, which were cooks and cleaners in the-- in the camp.

Then we're wondering that-- camp that we left to be-- be alive. There was only kids in such an age, because you never saw such, you know-- for years and years, you never saw kids, Jewish kids to be alive.

So they kept-- they looked after us for a while, because the-- it was a bit more to eat. We mostly do easier work.

INT: What had you been eating up till then?

EK: We had-- in the camp with-- in first camp for when the SS took-- in 1943 till 1944, nothing else-- just cabbage soup, cabbage [INAUDIBLE], cabbage, every day by day. And they had one horse for 1,000 people.

INT: One horse?

EK: One horse. There came a horse, where they brought the horse. And they skinned the horse, and the meat it was brought for the, you know, in the-- to eat.

And this other camp in the steel industry, it wasn't so bad. It was potatoes, some potato soup-- a bit of potatoes in the soup-- once a day, soup and bread. You'd get a quarter of bread-- a quarter slice, a quarter of a bread. But you used to get in a-- the bread, it could have been a kilo, like 1/2 kilo of weight.

Well, after four weeks, they decided-- I mean, the-- they needed men-- people-- in the munition factory. So they decided to take from here-- from this camp-- I don't know how many. It was about 20 or 30 of us.

I didn't-- I wasn't in the group. My one cousin was in the group to been taken to the other thing. So nobody wanted to-- it was a very bad camp, there, in the munition factory, so nobody wanted to go there.

So one boy, I exchanged with him, because I wanted to be together with my cousin. So we came to this-- daytime, they-- it took us a whole day from one camp to another camp to go, like, walk there, you know.

That was around this-- in October sometime. And we walked all day-- started by, at nighttime. We had to go to work in-- started the-- the time, it started at 7 o'clock in the evening till 7 o'clock in the morning, this-- the work-- you know-- how do you call this-- the shift.

Started, and so straight away, we came. 7 o'clock, they put us in the shift, straight away, to learn to manage the machine, munition factory machine. You came home. All-- I mean, you-- all day, you were on the road. And then on straight away, it's all night.

And the, you know, SS was going all the time, all the time around. If you tried to sleep, they'd hit you on the, on the head, in the back. And the-- you know--

INT: Did you get hit, there?

EK: Oh, yes. Oh, yes, because you're trying, you want to sleep. [NON-ENGLISH], the-- the fellow, that was-- fellow that's working the machine, said-- get up, but you know, sometimes, you couldn't help it. You'd-- so he came, and you'd get a hit on the head, on the back of the head.

INT: And, and that walk was-- that was a full day walk?

EK: A full day work-- walk from one-- one camp to another camp.

INT: Within the same--

EK: Same city, yeah. Same city. From one end-- it was like other suburbs, from-- out from the city. The factory, or the steel industry was out from the city.

INT: Mhm.

EK: But it belonged to this city. And so we had to walk a whole day.

INT: Did you see any of the locals when you were walking through?

EK: Yes, but they didn't-- I think, we'd see it was the-- the-- you know, the front and the back was-- on the side, SS, you know. You couldn't-- you had to walk. One-- one was left behind, so that, you know, you couldn't-- who couldn't make it, so--

INT: Did you stop at all during the day? You just kept walking?

EK: Just kept walking. One fellow, he couldn't keep walking, so-- [SIGHS] so that he didn't, he didn't make it. So we came to this munition factory.

INT: So this one-- how did, how did you keep walking? How did you find the strength to keep walking?

EK: Oh, it's-- you had to. The will to live-- so you walked. You didn't run, just walked, fast walk, ordinary walk. Because if the front one was the fastest, so he had to go faster.

But you know, he-- he is, on purpose, the fellow in the front made it slower that it's not to, not to make us run.

INT: Was there-- were you able to talk at all, throughout the line?

EK: No. You had to be quiet. If somebody had talked, he got belted. So we didn't-- no, we didn't talk, no.

INT: So do you remember, sort of, what sorts of things you'd be thinking about when you'd be walking?

EK: Oh, we're thinking, this is very bad, because they used to-- because those people, which we-- I used to work in the steel industry, they know about this factory-- I mean, this ammunition factory-- what's going on.

It was very bad, very-- you see, it was not-- no wires, only walls all around, high walls. You couldn't see nothing, what's going on out from this, from the thing, from the camp. You couldn't see nothing.

You couldn't see nothing. You couldn't hear nothing. You just, you know, you come, you-- the-- this, before the war, there used to be the horse-- where they had the horses, you know-- stable, horse stable.

That's where they made us to sleep in there with the wooden things. And there was-- and the factory was-- it used to be a textile factory once. So they brought in the machines from wherever they were, from the munition factories.

So they brought in, and we had to-- first night there, and I worked. Second night, already I went to sleep for the daytime. The shift was 12 hours, a 12-hour shift.

On-- on the second-- third night, already, they put me to the machine to work.

INT: What sort of work were you doing?

EK: I was with bullets. We used to make bullets for the-- for the-- for the guns. And the first night-- I mean third night, already, they put me with a machine. And I had to work.

I had to watch, you know, to put the-- machine was going up and down. And you had to make your normal. No, how do you call it-- normal? Your-- so much and so much you had to do a shift.

If you didn't do it, so you have to go next, in the morning, called to the SS. And you'd get 25 on your backside, if you didn't do it. But I wasn't-- my luck, I was at a good machine, so but I made it over norm-- over the-- what I was supposed to do, so I gave it to somebody else-- my-- that nobody is, you know.

But then one day, there came a fellow from Germany. And he had a stopwatch and the this and that. And he made it that what we did, in normal, that it was we had to make double what it was.

You make double. Oh, it was-- then-- then it was very bad, because if somebody-- if the machine broke down, your machine broke down-- you had lead. It was-- I don't know how-- 50 kilo lead was what they were using for their machine, for the bullets.

You have to go-- while the machine was broken, you had to go, put it on your shoulder, and you'd go around and carry it all the way, all the time, till the machine is fixed,

Then even when you didn't do it-- when if something happened, you know, you had to do it. If you didn't do it, you'd get up, get five on your lashes, on your backside.

My cousin-- because at once, he had-- here, I was in the different position-- different machine, and he had a different machine. And his machine was broken down quite often, and he got, once, he got-- they took him up to the office.

In front of everybody-- you know, the office was in the top-- and he got two lashes. He fainted-- put him under the water, under the tap, and gave him another two lashes, and then he went there.

INT: Ugh.

EK: Yeah.

INT: So and what exactly was the work that you were doing? What did you have to do?

EK: I had a, I had a bullet-- there, that point, the bullet which flies out from the body, from the, the top from the bullet.

INT: Mhm.

EK: I had to, I had to do it. I had to do it about a half of 500,000, something. It's 5-- I do not remember, now. But my, I had a machine, very good. But one day, the bullet came the wrong way and exploded and blocked pieces.

And I got one into the eye and one into the-- next to the ear, the nose. And I got-- got blind. Straight away, they took me-- I mean, it was bleeding from here, from the face.

And they took me to the doctor. The doctor just took the steel out from the eye, which was right into the eye.

INT: Actually, in the eyeball?

EK: In the eyeball, yeah. But they didn't bust it. What was in, in the eye-- through the skin, went into the eye-- he took it out. He took another steel out from, from next to the nose, which was a nerve.

And he said, that's all I can do for you. He gave me a black patch. And then-- then I didn't work till I-- because it happened somewhere around 4 o'clock or something-- late afternoon. Till 7 o'clock, I didn't do work.

But I stayed there one night. I had the black patch. The next day, I had to go back to work. And because if you didn't-- if the-- you didn't go to work, you had to report to the SS. And the SS-- you're not able to work, out you go.

INT: And you knew that was what would happen?

EK: Yes, I knew this. We, every, all-- everybody knew this. This is the-- the-- it's the law-- law-- law-- a rule that if nobody is able to work, he has to be reported to SS, and they put you to somewhere.

INT: And did you lose any friends like that?

EK: No, I-- I just, you know, when I had the patch, I-- I was a blind, fully blind-- black, couldn't see nothing. But then I was-- I was lucky, also. I had a good German foreman. He was a-- he was-- he was-- one foot shorter, you know.

And he, so he gave it to me-- you know, he didn't put me to a machine, but he put me to-- with the machine needed the-- the goods there to put in the machine, so I had to go give it to-- put it in the lead, or the steel for-- for the-- for the-- for the bullets.

So I, I did that for-- till one week, two weeks, I did this job. Then I went back to the machine.

INT: And so was there a big difference between the German foremen and the SS?

EK: Yes, it was a different. Yeah, because this was a civilian German. And he had, also, pity on me, because I was blind of this. So he, sometimes, he used to bring me a sandwich from-- from-- from his sandwich.

But he gave me a bit of easy work for-- till I was able to go back to machine. But after five days, you know, I was fully blind. It was only very small, dark-- yeah, a red, I could see.

So I-- I went to see the doctor and asked him whether-- if I will be blind? And I said, I can see, really, in there, far, like, far away, red. He said, that's a good sign that you'll be all right.

But the-- I was-- next to me, a few weeks after, it happened to another girl machinist. She wasn't very lucky. The steel went right through the eye. And she was-- you know, she lost her eye.

INT: Right.

EK: But this was a very bad camp, because, as I told you, it was walls and food was ersatz-- you know, ersatz potatoes. You know, they're made from I don't know what. It-- it burns you. The food, it was burning, burning you.

The food, the potatoes-- they were made of ersatz. I don't know what kind of, you know-- ersatz, that just means instead of something, you know.

INT: What type?

EK: Instead of a real potato, it was some made from a-- chemically, that was not a potato-- what, what is a potato. And it would burn you. It was very bad food. Very, you know--

INT: That was all you had to eat?

EK: Yeah, that's all what I had, every day and night. And at night, when you worked the shift work, you used to get-- at 12 o'clock at night, you used to get a half hour break to eat. That's what you got, and back to the machine-- work.

INT: What happened If you needed to go to the toilet? What would happen?

EK: It, you had to ask the man, which was carrying-- for seconds to do-- watch. You had to-- but you had to come back. They-- they looked at how long you were staying there.

Yeah, once-- or sometimes, it happened, or a few times, it happened, you worked all night with the machine. Next-- some time in the morning, next-- all day, you had to load the munitions in the wagons.

While you work all night, all day, you had to load on them, on the wagons-- and the next night, again. So you had 36 hours, just non-stop without the sleep, you had to work, you know.

INT: What was it like-- like, knowing what, what the work you was-- what you were doing was used for?

EK: Oh, we had, we had the control, you know-- that if somebody did something wrong, you had to-- you had all kinds-- it was-- I don't know how many thousand people used to work, there, because every part, we had 20 machines.

And every one is different work, different done, different works-- different testing and this and that. The filling, we didn't do the filling the powder. There was somebody in-- other, else, because that's-- people didn't last very long in this filling, because you had to fill with-- by hand.

And nothing on the mouth, you know. You'd eat the whole dust of the-- from the powder. And the whole body would get yellow. You used to get yellow. I was-- we were lucky that we didn't work in this place. People only lasted for six months.

And the-- but the-- a lot-- I was still-- some of this-- end of the-- just December, I was still working there, in this munition factory. You just came, you worked, and there was no rest.

You had, on Sunday, between the shift-- because when you changed from one shift to another shift, so you had a bit of rest-- 12 hours. And the rest of them-- every day, 12 hours, 12 hours.

INT: And--

EK: Once-- one week, morning shift-- day shift, one week, night shift.

INT: Did, did you talk at all between shifts, like, with the rest of the--

EK: Oh, yes. We'd talk between shifts, you know. We'd sit down-- when you'd eat, sit down on the floor and talk to each other.

INT: What were the bigger things that you talked about?

EK: Well, the biggest things we would would talk about-- what would be eat, what would we eat, when we will-- would be, if-- you know, thinking of food, thinking of what, what's going-- what you're going to eat when I will get free of this, if I will get free.

INT: Was it if, or was it when?

EK: If.

INT: If?

EK: If and when, because it-- already in 19-- it's-- in, you see, in 1943, the-- the Germans-- I mean, the Russians started to come in '44-- come nearer and nearer all the time.

So the-- we know-- we heard from the-- because in, and when they-- in the factory, there was also Polish people, non-Jewish. There were, you know, like, the mechanics there had been-- there had been-- or the Polish women, they came as a cook-- cook for the SS, you know.

So they told us what was going-- a little bit, what was going on in the war. Like, you know, because you didn't have no radio, no nothing.

INT: Mhm.

EK: Not allowed. So the Polish people, they sometimes told us what's going on as far the front-- we had the Russian going, with the English going, when they landed in '44, in Normandy.

And you know, this-- where they're bombing the German cities. That's-- they told us, sometimes, you know, because they had radios at home, because we didn't-- you know, I mean, that's how we-- that's how we got it-- connection with the outside the world.

INT: And did you notice any change in the Germans as they were getting closer?

EK: No. They-- they were worse. They were getting-- started, you know, more and more. As-- in 1944, they started to bring, from Hungary, from Romania, the Jews to Auschwitz.

So we saw the transports going, you know. Even in-- yeah, I forgot to tell you-- in 1942, when we were in this first camp, we were not far from the train line, on the train line. Just the train was here-- and below the train line was a camp.

And we saw the Dutch Jews in nice wagons with things. All-- they, they thought they were going to nice-- you know, they came with nice wagons-- not the-- like a-- like a passenger train.

Not like we came-- with a goods train. They came in the passenger train with everything, with the-- they thought they were going to-- but they took them straight away to Treblinka in 1942.

INT: And you used to watch them go past?

EK: Yeah, we watched them go past. Also-- also, when it was the-- in ghetto, it was in-- in Warsaw, when it was 1943, and it was the-- you know, how do you call this-- [NON-ENGLISH], you called it in German-- when it was the ghetto, we heard the fighting in ghetto.

So the-- we-- we lived in the-- from between the-- our camp was between Warsaw and the Majdanek, in Lublin. We were on the train. This is the main line that we used.

So we saw the-- one early mornings, when it was the-- the-- from the ghetto, when they was liquidating the Warsaw ghetto, it was a-- it came, passing through-- the train. And boys used to jump out from the, from the train, from the--

It was, because it was a foggy morning, foggy things. But still, was shooting all the time, but still was jumping. And the Germans, they gave the Polish people-- if they find a Jew, they'd get a kilo of sugar and a dozen eggs.

And a lot of Polish people did it. Found the boys, lying there on the ground, because if you'd get injured or something, so they'd give it up to the Germans.

INT: Hmm.

EK: [SIGHS] Yeah.

INT: Meanwhile, back at the factory, so how long were you there?

EK: In the factory, I was there from-- in this factory, I was there from September till the end of December. At the end of December, they took us where-- you know, they had-- have on the numbers. They didn't know no names or nothing.

Then, there, just the number-- this, this number should be coming this, this place-- to Appell place. And they put us in a-- we came, about 1,000 men came to this platz.

They put us on the trains, a few trains-- I mean, a few wagons.

INT: Were you, were you pleased to be leaving?

EK: We wasn't so pleased. We didn't know-- we had to. It was no, no choice. We had to, because the-- you know, they came. You had your number. They came to take you and put you there in the place. If you didn't-- shoot you on the spot.

So everybody was frightened. That's, you know-- everybody, everybody had the feeling of, hopefully, let's hope that we could get through to the war. So they did everything just to get over.

So we came there. They took us on the train-- also marching from-- you know, I mean, I didn't have no shoes, no nothing. You had the wooden sandals. So because there was snow and we would have to march.

So we'd get some piece of paper in the socks-- fill in the-- against the cold.

INT: When did you lose your shoes?

EK: I didn't lose, because they didn't give me. They didn't give me any shoes-- only gave me the wooden sandals that we used to have, you know, to work. No shoes-- sandals, the-- only the wooden ones on the bottom and a piece of leather on the top.

And that's how they used to go to work-- I-- I, because I didn't have shoes. Who had the shoes had the shoes. If you didn't have, you got these sandals with the things.

INT: Right.

EK: So I-- I got the socks and with a piece of paper around to keep me warm. And they took us about, also, 2 kilometers to the train-- also, guarding-- the SS with the guns and everything.

In-- and-- and the whole day, we-- we-- you know, we stayed on the train. At nighttime, they took us away. And next morning, they got free, and we were taken away from-- from the camp.

And the next morning, the Russians came there in the city.

INT: When did you find that out?

EK: We find it out-- you see, we, when we came on the train, we were three days traveling in Germany-- you know, stop, go, stop, because there were no trains. Three days it took us on the-- on the-- on the train.

Then we arrived to Buchenwald after three days.

INT: Did you have any food on the train?

EK: On the-- yeah. We had the-- they gave us food-- one bread for the whole way, all the way. That's all, and only bread.

INT: How many people in the wagon?

EK: Oh, about 100, 120-- something like that. You know, just, just to stick, standing up in this-- at nighttime, you know, we used to lean to get-- I mean, down, sit down-- not-- and you can't lie down-- only--

INT: Caught?

EK: Caught, you know, just like sitting down.

INT: What was the general feeling of everybody there? Was it-- were people-- was there a lot of anger or violence or--

EK: It was-- no, it was-- there was no violence. Everybody-- you know, you just go on and on and on. It was-- you try-- somebody tried to, you know-- we tried to open the, the floor. But you-- with nothing-- no knives, no nothing, you didn't have no-- you didn't have nothing-- any-- empty, everything empty.

INT: And was the food equally shared?

EK: Bread, that's all what you're getting-- bread.

INT: So you each got your own bread?

EK: Yeah, everybody got his own bread. And that's what-- because then-- I think they knew that it will be just three days. After, when we came to Buchenwald, then we saw what-- what goes on.

Then somebody in the-- in Buchenwald, there was already a radio, because Buchenwald was a very old camp. And there it was-- it's a different-- it was the old camp-- what you would have-- because it was, you know, from the old communist, German communists and all kinds there.

They had-- there were blocks of nice homes, there, everything, because they were-- came from 1938. We came in '45-- '44, '45. They put us right there on the bottom in the wooden barracks.

From the beginning-- well, we-- sorry, when we came to Buchenwald, you had to go undress everything, taken off-- the rings, everything-- nothing, just empty. And you came to a other side from the camp.

You know the, you know where the shower and everything, it was, you came other side, you got your clothing. If it wasn't yours, somebody-- you know, if it was-- fit you, it's good-- not fit you, just keep on going.

So we came to this camp where we-- first, we were eight to a-- eight to a bed. It's not a bed-- you know, the bleacher. You call these, you call these bleacher. They're like a-- things-- six to a-- six-- eight to a things.

Gradually, came more. More people came from Auschwitz, came from some Polish, from other camps. So there came 12-- eight like this, and four like this, sleep. You couldn't-- you--

