EK: Yeah. When we came the-- on the block, there were-- they give us from beginning that we have eight to ones loaf of bread. We didn't have no knives or nothing, no-- to cut, so it was always arguments. But we came a-- also boys, we-- I don't know how many boys, a bus came there, about 20 boys.

But in this block was a kapo, a Russian fellow. He was there since already-- I don't know, for many years. He had a knife. So he took all the boys, cut the bread for 10-- I mean eight, for eight slices. So he give everybody each. And that's-- you get this one slice for the whole day.

And you-- and in lunchtime was a soup, ersatz soup. That's also with water and then some powder in inside. That's all the whole-- we get the whole-- but then we-- the-- with-- we're not far from Weimar when it was bombed. We-- they started to send people after work. This is a city.

But this-- what-- it-- it-- the 4 o'clock the morning started to wake up everybody, up, up, up. So we-- we had to get up 4 o'clock in the morning, come back 11:00-- till 7 o'clock in the evening. But 7 o'clock 11 o'clock they start to count, count, and just to make suffering.

We-- yes, I wanted to tell also. We, we are eight to a block. Then the fourteen came from, from Auschwitz, and the-- then we started to get 10 people to a bread. Gradually less and less you get what to eat. But this soup sometime, you know, people-- we had daily taken out from the dead bodies what it was in the block-- in the, in the-- in the-- in the block.

Every-- during the night was mostly about 60 bodies out on the front--

INT: How many-- how many people would be in the block?

EK: In the block it was 3,800-some-- over 4,000 sometime.

INT: Oh.

EK: But as the-- soon people died, so they're less and less. People couldn't sleep. It was not enough place to-- beds to sleep. People standing there, standing in-- in the camp, in the block.

After, the kapos decided that they make it for the children, for young kids and boys another block. So they put us in another block, [NON-ENGLISH]. But we-- this was a-- but in meantime-- well, before they did it, we sleeped in the big block. Was-- but you couldn't go from one block to another-- from one camp to another block because the wires. You couldn't go through.

And so they put us another block, another camp. I mean, same camp, but the wires divided from one block to another blocks. So that we went other block. Wasn't so bad because only kids only. In this block was a kapo, was befriended with the SS. So the SS-- he ask the SS not to send us outside on this-- on the, on the snow. Kept inside in the, in the things.

INT: Wow.

EK: So the SS was ready to do it because he was friendly with this kapo. In other ones you had to stand-- before we went to the children's block, we had to stand in air outside, and in snow, till they count and again they count and they again count. Was no different. Just to make suffering.

INT: What was it like just to stand there?

EK: Cold. Shivering. Very cold. You see, Buchenwald is right on top in the mountain. Was-- was the-- as I said, daily was 60 night, day-- three times a day they came to collect the bodies.

INT: Did you know when somebody was going to die?

EK: Was a-- a dysentery.

INT: Right.

EK: It was a kind of dysentery which blood came out.

INT: Right.

EK: People lasted only two days, and they died. I--

INT: Were you scared that you were gonna get it, or--

EK: Pardon? I was lucky. No. The people-- mostly people which lived on-- on floors, right, they did it-- got it be-- from the cold. I-- my-- they put us on the third stage, block, from the floor. So we didn't bad-- so bad there.

But the food were-- hunger, was very big hunger with-- people die from hunger. People die from hunger most of the time. That's what it is, was hunger and-- and suffering. That's what mostly people die. And from the-- the sickness, the-- the dysentery.

But then we were taken in other camp, the children's camp. That was OK. There was no, no-- not much sickness there because we didn't go standing outside there form Appell. We only stayed inside.

But one day we went in-- in entlausen with the shower. We-- once a week we had to go for shower, for things. And somehow we saw bodies there without the hand and feet, just a torso. So the cousin, my cousin, he fainted. He saw those things. He couldn't take it and fainted.

So the German doctor-- he-- he was a, a-- from the house, not the SS, a German doctor. And he took him number that he-- that he, you know-- that's he-- he gets worse, very bad. So he help him, take him to hospital. And hospital was in better condition than in-- in-- in the camp. Hospital you had one, your own bed.

And my cousin was-- all of a sudden he got a pneumonia, double pneumonia. So they took him to hospital. He was in the hospital just a few weeks-- a few months before we got free. So he got pneumonia. I took him to the hospital there. They didn't have nothing to-- what to give him.

There was a Jewish doctor. A real doctor. He was at home. A Czech fellow, Jewish. The-- he was a doctor. But he did nothing to give. And there was another Czech fellow. With him was another doctor. He was a-- things. But the, the Jewish doctor told him, listen here, I can't-- I have nothing to-- what to give him.

Only you want to save this child, you only can stay with him. Give him a cold on his forehead to keep the temperature down. And he did. This fellow did it day and night. So it kept him alive. This is my cousin.

INT: Wow.

EK: And only I didn't see him for a few months because we couldn't go to the hospital. We couldn't go from one place to another place. So I didn't see him at all.

INT: Was that the first time you'd been separated?

EK: The first time we been separated, yeah. The first few months before he went into the hospital there.

INT: What was it like being apart?

EK: Was very hard because, you know, if we have two together-- because when I went to work to Weimar, I used to bring something to eat, and he-- one day I went. One day he went. Because it's, you know, as I told you, from 7 o'clock-- from 11 o'clock at night till 4 o'clock morning, that's what you had sleep.

And you had-- you get up and count and you go to the city, Weimar, which was 20, 30 kilometers by train, packed. But we didn't mind to go because of the food. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] But then when he went in hospital, I-- I couldn't-- I didn't go to work anymore because they didn't put us on. And they give us bread.

Five days before the American came, the-- they didn't give us nothing. They blew up the water pipe. The water wasn't come in anymore in the camp. And they started to collect people from our from the-- from our blocks first, from the Jewish people, you know, the bottom part of the camp. Started to take them on the death march.

For five days they didn't give nothing. For five days they took already our block, kids, when we, when we-- so we all went on the fifth-- fifth day. That I went to the-- up to the Appellplatz slowly. Couldn't walk because weakness.

Some kids was too-- too quick, so they took them behind the gates. And what we left-- what left, we stayed on the Appellplatz because the-- the-- close-- the American plane came so the SS run away, and they close the gates. And we stayed there on the few-- I don't know how many boys. Half or quarter of the boys, left them behind on the Appellplatz.

Then we came-- somebody came, some-- with the purple there, and some priest, Jehovah Witness fellow came to tell us to move slowly back. Maybe tomorrow you'll be free. So we start to move slowly back to the camp. But the--

INT: What was it like when you were walking back then after?

EK: Was very weak. I was very weak. Very-- couldn't walk. I mean, it's-- just made it to-- back to the camp. Stay there overnight in the-- in bed. Next morning we heard this start shooting was going on from the-- the guards start to shooting in-- into the camp.

And somebody said-- it was, it was talking that somebody got hurt with the American, from-- they were 20 kilometers from us in [NON-ENGLISH]. They were sitting there for a few days, the American troops. And they're sitting, and somebody somehow send a message. Made it from some wires, from-- the Americans, that's we-- they're liquidating the camp.

So the Americans started to move. The SS, the German SS, run away. And they left only the-- you know, the Hungarian, Romanians, and the Ukrainian SS watching the things. The people from the top in the camp, the-- was the old ones, they started to move around. So the SS start to shoot because somebody was going to put the white flag on the top, on the mine-- the tower, what there is, you know.

When the-- I didn't-- I just came out from the block, sit on the outside in the sun because I couldn't move. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] We-- we saw the tanks, American tanks, coming afternoon. Then my cousin came.

[PAUSES FOR 17 SECONDS]

Came to-- [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] he came to see me. I was lying there in the floor. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I couldn't move. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] He saw me. He saw me. He start to cry and I start to cry. We were happy to see the-- [PAUSES FOR 11 SECONDS] [DRINKS]

We were happy that we got free. So I-- we-- he went to-- back to camp. He's-- to hospital. And he's-- he knew that we didn't eat bread-- didn't eat any food for five days, no drink. So he went to the hospital, and he got the-- from the fellow slice of bread. He brought it to me. I couldn't finish it. I couldn't eat because everything was dry. Then he went to get-- get me some water to put it in. [SIGHS]

Then at nighttime the-- when the-- we got freed already. SS was gone. So the fellows from the camp, top camp, they killed the pigs what there was in-- what left from the SS. The Germans had to bring water to cook this soup. The first night we had a soup from-- made from the-- from the pigs.

We ate. I ate. We-- everybody ate because they were hungry. But then lots of-- all of a sudden lots of boys got sick from overeating. They were vomiting and dysentery and everything. To my luck I wasn't sick. I wasn't nothing.

I was-- I was-- but the next day I couldn't eat nothing for a while. But in the evening I start to eat again. After three days came-- the Americans came. They took me to the doctor. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] First it was DDT. Then the-- bit by bit, one by one, every boy had to go to the doctor. And-- and I, you know-- they put us in the SS camp, in the home, to stay there, to live.

And I had to go every day to, to the doctor. I used to get a tablet, the iron tablet, to-- things to get up. After three days I started to eat normally, bit by bit. I used to get-- you know, American came. They start to cook. Or I used to be on the milk diet. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] To-- to get-- [SIGHS] and then we had a-- then I was, you know, free.

We-- my cousin, he still were-- the-- he was already in-- all right. He got--

INT: Well, what--

EK: Or-- he got all right. So we stayed together. We came together. And all of a sudden he got sick again. He get the pneumonia again by the Americans. So there came the-- we stay-- I stayed there till August in, in Buchenwald by the American.

INT: What was it like when the Americans came? What was the feeling?

